---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE } from '../site.config';
import { STATS } from '../data/games';

const title = 'About Hunt Games';
const description =
  'What the Hunt Games network is, how a wiki gets surveyed and catalogued, and how the whole thing is run.';
const updated = '2026-08-18';

const schema = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'AboutPage',
    name: title,
    url: new URL('/about/', SITE.url).href,
    publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: SITE.name, url: SITE.url },
  },
];
---

<Base title={title} description={description} updated={updated} schema={schema} ogType="article">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
    <h1>About Hunt Games</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Hunt Games is a small network of independent, fan-made wikis. Each site covers exactly one
      game, in depth, at its own subdomain under hunt-games.com. This page is the network&rsquo;s
      index &mdash; {STATS.total} entries catalogued, {STATS.live} of them live.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="wrap prose">
    <h2>Why one site per game</h2>
    <p>
      Most game wikis try to cover everything at once and end up thin everywhere. Each Hunt Games
      site does the opposite: it exists because we found measured search demand for that specific
      game, and it only publishes pages that answer a real question someone is asking &mdash;
      release dates, mechanics that aren&rsquo;t explained in-game, calculators, build guides,
      whatever the game itself actually withholds from the player.
    </p>

    <h2>How an entry gets here</h2>
    <p>Every listing on the index goes through the same three stages:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Surveyed.</strong> We research what people actually search for about the game
        before writing a single page, so nothing gets published without a reason to exist.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Catalogued.</strong> Once the site is live at its own subdomain, it&rsquo;s added
        to this index with a genre, a status, and a link.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Maintained.</strong> Sites are periodically re-checked for broken links, stale
        release information, and guides that patches have made incorrect.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="disclaimer">Not affiliated with the games we cover</h2>
    <p>
      Hunt Games and every site in the network are independent fan projects. We are not affiliated
      with, endorsed by, or sponsored by any of the studios or publishers whose games are indexed
      here. Game names, logos, screenshots, and other assets referenced across the network belong
      to their respective owners and are used under fair use for commentary and reference. See the{' '}
      <a href="/terms/">terms of use</a> for the full statement.
    </p>

    <h2>Suggesting a game</h2>
    <p>
      If there&rsquo;s a game you think deserves the same treatment &mdash; a real, sourced,
      single-purpose wiki &mdash; <a href="/contact/">tell us</a>. We can&rsquo;t promise a site for
      every suggestion, but we read every one.
    </p>

    <h2>Corrections</h2>
    <p>
      Something wrong on one of the network&rsquo;s sites, or in this index itself? Email{' '}
      <a href={`mailto:${SITE.contactEmail}`}>{SITE.contactEmail}</a> and say which page. Each
      individual wiki also lists its own contact details.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
